</strong>, far fewer than the 74 that chance alone would give, and <strong class=\"sig\">179 record highs</strong>, far more than the 74 that chance alone would give. The 2010s hold the most record highs of any decade (72). Reno's all-time high is 108\u00b0F, on July 10, 2002.","t_chg":"Reno has warmed, days and nights alike","p_chg":"Across 128 complete years: Reno's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.44\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.28 to +0.59), about +5.6\u00b0F since 1896. Reno's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.44\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.38 to +0.51), about +5.7\u00b0F since 1896.","t_mon":"Lows rose in all 12 months. Highs rose in 11 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Reno's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (\u22120.01 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 7.3 inches a year. The wettest year was 2017 (13.73 in); the driest, 1947 (1.55 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":true},"t_year":"2026 so far: 18 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}